  "worked_solution": "The local truncation error (LTE) is the error made in a single step of a numerical method, when the step begins from an exact value. The LTE of a numerical method at step $n$ from $x$ with exact value ${y}_{\\text{exact}}(x)$ to ${x}_{\\text{new}}=x+\u0394x$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\u03c4}_{n} & ={y}_{\\text{exact}}({x}_{\\text{new}})-{y}_{\\text{new}} \\\\  & ={y}_{\\text{exact}}({x}_{\\text{new}})-({y}_{\\text{exact}}(x)+\u0394y).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that the exact solution ${y}_{\\text{exact}}(x)=3{e}^{-x}.$ So, the $y$-value at the start of the step $x=1$ is ${y}_{\\text{exact}}(1)=3{e}^{-1}.$\n\nNow, let's find the $y$-increment given by the numerical method.\n\nFirst, the slope at the start of the step is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{exact}}^{\u2032}(1) & =-{y}_{\\text{exact}}(1) \\\\  & =-3{e}^{-1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the increment at this step is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\u0394y & ={y}_{\\text{exact}}^{\u2032}(1)\\cdot \u0394x \\\\  & =-3{e}^{-1}\\cdot 0.2 \\\\  & =-0.6{e}^{-1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we compute the exact value at ${x}_{\\text{new}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{exact}}({x}_{\\text{new}}) & ={y}_{\\text{exact}}(x+\u0394x) \\\\  & ={y}_{\\text{exact}}(1+0.2) \\\\  & ={y}_{\\text{exact}}(1.2) \\\\  & =3{e}^{-1.2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the LTE for the step starting at $x=1:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\u03c4}_{6} & ={y}_{\\text{exact}}(1.2)-({y}_{\\text{exact}}(1)+\u0394y) \\\\  & =3{e}^{-1.2}-(3{e}^{-1}-0.6{e}^{-1}) \\\\  & =3{e}^{-1.2}-2.4{e}^{-1} \\\\  & \u22480.021\\end{aligned}\n$$",
